                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>What's the difference between tip pooling and tip sharing?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Tip pooling is a system where all or a percentage of tips collected during a shift are aggregated into a single pool and then redistributed among eligible employees based on a set formula (like hours worked or points). Tip sharing (or tipping out) is a practice where tipped employees give a portion of their individual tips to support staff (like bussers, food runners, or barbacks) who helped them perform their duties.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>What is a tip pooling calculator?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>A tip pooling calculator is a digital tool designed to automate the process of calculating tip distributions for restaurant and hospitality workers. It eliminates math errors by taking inputs like total tips, hours worked, or role points, and calculating the exact cash share for each staff member in accordance with federal and state compliance rules.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>Who should use a tip pooling calculator?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Restaurant owners, general managers, shift leads, bar managers, and hospitality supervisors should use a tip pooling calculator to save time at the end of shifts, maintain complete payroll transparency, and ensure compliance with FLSA laws. Shift employees can also use it to verify that their distributed tip payouts are mathematically accurate.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>How do you calculate tip pooling?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Calculating tip pooling depends on the chosen method. For an hours-based split, you divide the total tip pool by the sum of all hours worked to find the hourly tip rate, then multiply that rate by each individual's hours. For a points-based split, you assign point values to roles, multiply each employee's hours by their points to get point-hours, sum the point-hours to find the value per point-hour, and distribute tips proportionally.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>How should dispensaries divide tips?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Cannabis dispensaries generally split tips using either an equal split per shift or an hours-based calculation among budtenders on duty. Because dispensary compliance rules are strict under state cannabis regulations, managers must ensure that non-exempt budtenders receive 100% of the tips and that managers, supervisors, or security guards are excluded from participating in the tip pool.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>Can a manager or supervisor ever be part of a tip pool?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>No — this is one of the most common compliance mistakes. Under federal law (FLSA), managers and supervisors are never allowed to receive tips from a pool, even if they occasionally perform service work like bussing tables or bartending during a rush. This applies in every U.S. state. If a manager regularly does hands-on tipped work as their primary duty, consult an employment attorney — job title alone doesn't determine "manager" status under the law.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>Can kitchen staff (cooks, dishwashers) be included in a tip pool?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>It depends on whether your employer takes a "tip credit" — meaning they pay tipped employees less than full minimum wage and count tips toward the difference. If a tip credit is claimed, back-of-house staff generally cannot be included in the pool under federal law. If the employer pays full minimum wage with no tip credit, back-of-house inclusion is generally allowed federally — but some states add their own restrictions on top of this, so check your state's specific rules.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>Can California restaurants include kitchen staff (cooks, dishwashers) in a tip pool?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Generally yes — unlike most states, California doesn't allow employers to take a tip credit, so the federal restriction that blocks back-of-house inclusion when a tip credit is claimed doesn't apply here. Tip pool participants still need to meaningfully contribute to the service customers receive, so confirm current guidance from the California Labor Commissioner's Office before including purely non-customer-facing roles.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>Since Texas allows a tip credit, does that limit who can be in the tip pool?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes — this is the detail Texas restaurant managers most often miss. If your restaurant takes a tip credit (pays the lower tipped minimum wage), federal law prohibits including back-of-house staff like cooks and dishwashers in the pool. To legally include kitchen staff, the employer must pay the full Texas minimum wage with no tip credit taken.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>Are there differences in tip pooling rules between New York City and the rest of the state?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>The core eligibility rules are generally consistent statewide, but minimum wage and tip credit amounts differ by region — New York City, Long Island/Westchester, and the rest of the state each have their own figures under New York's Hospitality Wage Order. This regional split directly affects the wage math behind your tip pool calculations, so confirm your specific region's current numbers.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>Since Florida's minimum wage is different from the federal minimum, how does that affect the tip credit?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Florida employers taking a tip credit must ensure a tipped employee's cash wage plus tips reaches Florida's minimum wage — not the lower federal minimum — and Florida's minimum wage has been rising on a set schedule under a voter-approved measure. That means the tip credit gap employers need to cover has been shrinking year over year, so always check the current year's figure rather than reusing an old one.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>What's the difference between "tip pooling" and "tip sharing" or "tipping out"?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Tip pooling means all tips are collected and redistributed based on a set formula (equal split, hours, or role points). Tip sharing/"tipping out" is different — it's when an individual employee (like a server) voluntarily gives a portion of their own tips directly to support staff (like a busser or bartender), rather than tips being pooled centrally first. The two are regulated somewhat differently, and mixing up the terms can lead to confusion about which rules apply.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>Is it legal to include hosts in a tip pool?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>This varies more than people expect. Hosts are traditionally viewed as engaged in customer service, so many states allow their inclusion, but a few jurisdictions treat hosts differently depending on how much direct customer interaction the role involves. There's no single national answer — check your state labor department's specific guidance on which roles qualify as "customarily tipped."</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>Can my employer keep any portion of pooled tips?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>No, under federal law employers can never keep any part of employee tips for the business — this applies regardless of whether a tip credit is taken. This is a firm federal rule (not state-dependent) and violations can result in significant penalties.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>How often do these rules actually change?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>More often than most people expect — federal tip pooling regulations have been revised multiple times in recent years (notably around back-of-house inclusion rules), and states periodically update minimum wage and tip credit amounts, sometimes annually. This is exactly why we date and source our compliance data rather than presenting it as permanently fixed — always verify current rules before finalizing a policy.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
                
                <details class="faq-accordion">
                    <summary class="faq-question">
                        <span>I run a small café with only 3 employees — do these rules still apply to me?</span>
                        <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                            <path d="M4.5 6.75L9 11.25L13.5 6.75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Generally yes. Federal tip pooling rules under the FLSA apply based on whether employees are "tipped employees" and whether a tip credit is taken — not based on business size in most cases. However, some state-level wage laws do have small-business thresholds or exemptions, so it's worth checking your specific state's rules rather than assuming a small operation is automatically exempt.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
